\caption(Grid coordinates are consumed prefix lengths: j runs horizontally and i downward. The marked middle-row crossing is selected from actual tied profiles.)
\alttext(An alignment path for AB and BA crosses the middle row at column0 and finishes at(2,2), illustrating an optimal path different from dense diagonal traceback.)

> Derive the split from the global recurrence

Let H(i,j) be the best score for prefixes a[:i] and b[:j]. With s equal to match
or mismatch for the next byte pair and g the linear gap score,

$$H(i,j)=\max\{H(i-1,j-1)+s,\ H(i-1,j)+g,\ H(i,j-1)+g\},\quad H(i,0)=ig,\quad H(0,j)=jg.$$
\alttext(A global alignment either consumes a byte from both strings or consumes one byte opposite a gap. The boundaries penalize all unmatched prefix bytes.)

For k=⌊n/2⌋, let F(j) score a[:k] against b[:j], and R(j) score a[k:] against
b[j:]. Reverse traversal computes R without copying reversed strings. Any
complete monotone grid path visits row k at some column j. Its two parts cannot
score above F(j) and R(j), while concatenating optimal subpaths at any chosen j
realizes their sum. Therefore

$$H(n,m)=\max_{0\le j\le m}\{F(j)+R(j)\}.$$
\alttext(The optimum full alignment score is the maximum of forward and suffix scores at a middle-row crossing.)

Choosing the first maximizing j fixes the split tie rule. Recursion on the two
ranges reconstructs an optimum, with empty/one-character cases handled directly.
The one-character implementation preserves the old diagonal/up/left traceback
preference without allocating a dense base-case matrix. The proof applies to
these additive symmetric linear scores, including unusual positive gap rewards;
it does not cover affine gap-open scoring with extra states.

> Preserve the original DNA-string example

The original inputs are AGTACGCA and TATGC, with scores 2/−1/−2. The first
midpoint is 4 in the first string. The following program takes forward, suffix
and summed profiles directly from the actual first split record, alongside the
actual aligned output. Its dense reference is a separate native Needleman–Wunsch
call; equal scores do not require identical gapped rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hirschberg_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const std::string a = "AGTACGCA", b = "TATGC";
    const auto t = dm::algorithms::hirschberg_trace(a, b);
    const auto nw = dm::algorithms::needleman_wunsch(a, b);
    const auto &s = t.splits.at(0);
    std::print("Hirschberg score {}; rows `{}` / `{}`. Dense NW score {}; rows `{}` / `{}`.\n",
               t.run.alignment.score, t.run.alignment.a_aligned,
               t.run.alignment.b_aligned, nw.score, nw.a_aligned, nw.b_aligned);
    std::print("\n| global split column | forward | reverse suffix | actual sum |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | " "---: |\n");
    std::vector<double> j, f, r, total;
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < s.total.size(); ++k) {
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", s.b_begin + k, s.forward[k],
                   s.reverse[k], s.total[k]);
        j.push_back(k + s.b_begin);
        f.push_back(s.forward[k]);
        r.push_back(s.reverse[k]);
        total.push_back(s.total[k]);
    }
    std::print("\nChosen midpoint ({},{}); actual split records {}; rolling cells {}; base pair scans {}; " "peak live score slots {}; max recursive depth {}.\n",
               s.a_mid, s.b_mid, t.splits.size(), t.run.work.rolling_cells,
               t.run.work.base_pairs, t.run.work.peak_score_slots,
               t.run.work.max_depth);
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(j, f, "actual forward", {37, 99, 235}, 2.).line(
        j, r, "actual reverse suffix", {5, 150, 105}, 2.).line(j, total,
                                                               "actual sum",
                                                               {220, 38, 38}, 2.).points({double(s.b_mid)}, {double(s.total[s.b_mid - s.b_begin])}, "first maximum", {202, 138, 4}, 7.).title("Original DNA fixture: actual crossing profiles").x_label("column j in TATGC").y_label("integer alignment score").y_limits(-18, 14).save_svg("build/hirschberg_original.svg");
    if (t.run.alignment.score != nw.score || s.a_mid != 4)
        throw std::runtime_error("original score");
}
```
