> What linear score storage means in this implementation

A rolling sweep allocates two rows of m+1 scores. Its returned forward row
remains alive while a reverse sweep allocates two more, so the producing peak is
three live score rows, not only two. Both returned profiles are destroyed before
either recursive child is entered. Borrowed views replace substring/reversal
copies, and one output buffer pair is appended instead of recursively
concatenating alignment strings.

Legacy `hirschberg` retains the original input orientation, so its score-row
storage is O(|b|), even when b is longer. New run/trace calls default to
transposing the internal inputs when necessary to put the shorter string in the
score-row dimension. Results are returned in caller row order. This yields
O(min(n,m)) score values, plus O(log(max(n,m)+1)) recursive frames and O(n+m)
owned output. Transposition can change tied optimal rows but preserves the score
under symmetric scoring.

The next figure measures actual score-vector element peaks, not allocator
capacity, bytes or process RSS. Its two-value first string makes the orientation
distinction visible. Output length still grows with the longer input, and trace
histories are absent from this experiment. A second table checks selected
rolling/base work on balanced generated inputs, without timing claims.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/hirschberg_space.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    std::vector<double> x, oriented, legacy;
    std::print("| long second length | shorter-oriented peak scores | preserved-orientation peak scores | " "output columns | score agrees |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (int power = 4; power <= 11; ++power) {
        const auto n = std::size_t{1} << power;
        const std::string b(n, 'A');
        const auto a = dm::algorithms::hirschberg_run("AB", b);
        const auto old =
            dm::algorithms::hirschberg_run("AB", b, {}, {false, 0, 0, 0});
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.work.peak_score_slots,
                   old.work.peak_score_slots, a.alignment.a_aligned.size(),
                   a.alignment.score == old.alignment.score);
        x.push_back(power);
        oriented.push_back(std::log2(double(a.work.peak_score_slots)));
        legacy.push_back(std::log2(double(old.work.peak_score_slots)));
        if (a.work.peak_score_slots > 9 ||
            old.work.peak_score_slots != 3 * (n + 1) ||
            a.alignment.score != old.alignment.score)
            throw std::runtime_error("oriented row bound");
    }
    std::print("\n| balanced length | actual rolling cells | actual base pair scans | calls |\n| ---: | " "---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (int power = 3; power <= 8; ++power) {
        const auto n = std::size_t{1} << power;
        std::string a(n, 'A'), b(n, 'B');
        for (std::size_t j = 0; j < n; ++j) {
            if (j % 3)
                a[j] = 'B';
            if (j % 2)
                b[j] = 'A';
        }
        const auto r = dm::algorithms::hirschberg_run(a, b);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, r.work.rolling_cells,
                   r.work.base_pairs, r.work.calls);
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.line(x, oriented, "shorter rows", {37, 99, 235}, 2.).line(
        x, legacy, "legacy rows", {220, 38, 38}, 2.).title(
        "Actual live score slots: rows are released before children").x_label(
        "log2 longer second-input length").y_label("log2 live producing score-vector elements").y_limits(0, 16).save_svg("build/hirschberg_space.svg");
}
