\image(figures/hirschberg_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Positive gaps are allowed mathematically and can favor opposing inserted gaps. This is a scoring control, not a recommended biological scoring model or affine-gap implementation.)
\alttext(Bars show how the number of alignment columns changes as the gap score moves from penalty to zero to reward, alongside exact-cap and byte-domain checks.)

Calls share no internal mutable state. Output rows and split arrays own their
storage and copy independently; no borrowed input views survive a call. Split
coordinates refer to internally oriented inputs, with `transposed` making that
orientation explicit. Forward and suffix arrays, summed candidates and first
maximizing columns are actual producing records, not separately replayed
illustrations. Selected work excludes allocation, initialization, output writes,
profile-copy overhead and total machine operations.

> What this establishes

A middle-row crossing reduces global traceback to two score sweeps and smaller
subproblems. Correct score recovery permits different tied alignments, as the
tiny example demonstrates. Linear score-row storage requires releasing parent
profiles and treating input orientation deliberately; output, frames and
retained histories still require storage of their own. The original synthetic
DNA strings are the problem fixture here, so an unrelated dataset would add no
evidence.

The original source is D. S. Hirschberg (1975), [A Linear Space Algorithm for
Computing Maximal Common Subsequences](https://doi.org/10.1145/360825.360861),
Communications of the ACM 18(6),341–343; an [author-hosted
copy](https://ics.uci.edu/~dhirschb/pubs/p341-hirschberg.pdf) presents rolling
Algorithm B, the middle-split theorem and first-maximizer Algorithm C. That
paper develops longest common subsequences and sketches an edit-distance
adaptation. This library applies the decomposition principle to additive global
alignment scoring, with different boundary initialization and base
reconstruction.

Verification compares complete legacy alignments under all checked short binary
pairs and 27 scoring schemes, checks independently scored output rows and all
captured split profiles, and instruments live score/work counts under
sanitizers. A separate exact monotone-path enumeration checks short global
alignment optima without a rolling DP implementation. Domain, cap, orientation,
ownership, embedded-NUL and integer-bound controls accompany the report. API
examples, all complete programs, inspected figures and both exports are verified
before retiring the original report.
